# nanopb generator options for the minimal containerd API set (v1.12.0).
#
# STRATEGY (Wave 0-D, codegen contract):
#   Unbounded fields (string, bytes, repeated, map) become dynamically
#   allocated pointers (FT_POINTER) via `fallback_type`; the codecs and the
#   nanopb runtime are compiled with PB_ENABLE_MALLOC, so pb_decode allocates
#   and pb_release() frees. The C controller owns the allocator (libc malloc
#   on a -static binary) and must pb_release() every decoded message.
#
#   Why fallback_type:FT_POINTER instead of `type:FT_POINTER`:
#     `type` would force EVERY field (including fixed-size scalars like
#     uint32 pid / int64 seconds / bool / enums and embedded messages like
#     Timestamp) onto the heap, turning the generated structs into
#     pointer-everything with poor ergonomics for the assembly-driven
#     controller. `fallback_type` only kicks in where nanopb's static
#     allocation cannot work -- exactly the fields that are unbounded:
#       * string fields without max_size/max_length
#       * bytes fields without max_size/fixed_length
#       * repeated fields without max_count
#       * map fields (implemented as repeated map-entry messages)
#     and circular message dependencies (none here).
#     Scalar/enum fields stay plain C values; message fields stay embedded.
#     Unbounded fields become:
#       string           -> char *            (allocated, NUL-terminated)
#       bytes            -> pb_bytes_array_t * (allocated)
#       repeated T       -> pb_size_t count; T *items (allocated array)
#       map<K,V>         -> pb_size_t count; K_V_Entry *entries
#   This matches the repo's "you own the allocator" decision for the
#   containerd API: no size annotations are possible for arbitrary labels /
#   annotations / Any payloads, and callbacks would force per-field plumbing
#   in every controller RPC.

* fallback_type:FT_POINTER